{"name":"Acacia Tree","hex":"#a69d64","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"acacia","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A shrub or tree of the tribe Acacieae. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","The thickened or dried juice of several species in Acacieae, in particular Vachellia nilotica (syn. Acacia nilotica, Egyptian acacia)."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A roll or bag, filled with dust, borne by Byzantine emperors, as a memento of mortality. It is represented on medals."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/acacia"},{"term":"tree","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A perennial woody plant taller and larger than a shrub with a wooden trunk and, at some distance from the ground, having leaves and branches.","Any other plant (such as a large shrub or herb) that is reminiscent of the above in form and size."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To chase (an animal or person) up a tree.","To place in a tree."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tree"}],"entries":[{"title":"Acacia","blocks":[{"text":"Acacia, commonly known as wattles or acacias, is a genus of about 1,084 species of shrubs and trees in the subfamily Mimosoideae of the pea family Fabaceae. Initially, it comprised a group of plant species native to Africa, South America, and Australasia, but is now reserved for species mainly from Australia, with others from New Guinea, Southeast Asia, and the Indian Ocean."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Acacia_baileyana_3%2C_Canberra_ACT.JPG/330px-Acacia_baileyana_3%2C_Canberra_ACT.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acacia"},{"title":"Tree","blocks":[{"text":"In botany, a tree is a perennial plant with an elongated stem, or trunk, usually supporting branches and leaves. In some usages, the definition of a tree may be narrower, e.g., including only woody plants with secondary growth, only plants that are usable for timber, or only plants above a specified height. Wider definitions include taller palms, tree ferns, bananas, and bamboos."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Usamljeni_jasen_-_panoramio_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Usamljeni_jasen_-_panoramio_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tree"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Acacia_dealbata_kz09.jpg/500px-Acacia_dealbata_kz09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Acacia_dealbata_kz09.jpg/1280px-Acacia_dealbata_kz09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Acacia_dealbata_kz09.jpg/500px-Acacia_dealbata_kz09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bipinnate leaves of Acacia dealbata","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAcacia_dealbata_kz09.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Acacia_penninervis_%285368395701%29.jpg/500px-Acacia_penninervis_%285368395701%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Acacia_penninervis_%285368395701%29.jpg/1280px-Acacia_penninervis_%285368395701%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Acacia_penninervis_%285368395701%29.jpg/500px-Acacia_penninervis_%285368395701%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Phyllodes of Acacia penninervis","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAcacia_penninervis_(5368395701).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Ac